From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ios-blink-sideload <noreply@example.invalid>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 23:28:57 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] iOS sideload: site permissions for location, clipboard,
 camera, mic

The browser context had no permission delegate, so content denied
every permission without asking: geolocation failed with "User denied
Geolocation", reading and even writing the clipboard was
NotAllowedError, and permissions.query() said denied for everything.
Camera and microphone requests (WebContentsDelegate::
RequestMediaAccessPermission()) were denied too. And on iOS the device
service gets the location from Core Location, which needs the
embedder's GeolocationSystemPermissionManager; without one it would
CHECK-fail once a page was allowed the location.

Add ContentPermissionManager: writing to the clipboard is allowed, as
in Chrome; the location and reading the clipboard are asked for with an
alert, using the mobile strings components already has
(IDS_GEOLOCATION_INFOBAR_TEXT, IDS_CLIPBOARD_INFOBAR_TEXT,
IDS_PERMISSION_ALLOW, IDS_PERMISSION_DONT_ALLOW); everything else is
still denied. Answers are kept for the top-level origin until the app
quits; a page that is not visible is not asked. ContentWebState asks
for the camera and the microphone with the embedder's prompt, the one
WKWebView's requests get (WebStateDelegate::
HandlePermissionsDecisionRequest()), keeps the answer until another
page shows (as WebKit does; the prompt's "allow once" and "allow while
visiting" both come back as allow), and picks the requested devices (or
the first) as Chromecast does. IOSBrowserMainParts creates the system
location permission manager, as content_shell does on iOS.

Status: verified at Chromium 4558926c in the iOS 27 simulator with a
test page: permissions.query() says prompt for geolocation,
clipboard-read, camera and microphone, granted for clipboard-write and
denied for notifications; getCurrentPosition() shows the system
location prompt for the app, then "<site> wants to use your device's
location" (in Chinese, from the existing strings), and once allowed
returns the simulated location without asking again; readText() asks
the same way, then iOS asks to paste from the other app, and the text
comes back; denying makes readText() fail without asking again;
writeText() works without a prompt; with --use-fake-device-for-media-
stream (the simulator has no camera), getUserMedia() shows Chrome's
camera prompt, "allow once" gives a video track, a second request on
the page does not ask, and after a reload it asks again. Not verified
on a device: a real camera and microphone.

